rld  yeah i really think that the ukraine war  has shown a spotlight on where we get  our energy from um not just the uk but  globally  and i think that you know lots of  countries are actually doubling down on  their renewables investments because of  what's happening  in ukraine  and in particular  the eu for example  has leaked a document yesterday  that suggested it would be further  upping its own targets  and other countries are doing the same  simply because renewables provide that  cheap source of energy  which is more secure than oil and gas  which comes from places like russia  and i expect that other countries will  also look to bolster their renewable  sectors um as things remain spiky and  volatile in fossil fuel markets um it  really is a hell of an incentive for  people to move away from oil and gas  when it's this volatile and this  expensive yeah it's a volatile market a  days but would you agree there with jess  that the the war in ukraine has then  made  governments you know look at where they  are and how they can do better  definitely and i think in this sad time  of where we see so much suffering we can  see the full effects of what may happen  if you know more fuel is put on the fire  in terms of climate change so i do  definitely think that in times of crisis  we've been using this to find really  strong roots out and roots out that can  be solutions for the future as well and  this is why i think going and looking  for pure forms and more green forms of  energy is just necessary  okay well i want to move on to another  subject now um extinction rebellion have  responded to the government's plan clamp  down on climate process by saying  they'll get millions of people onto the  streets um is there a risk of a backlash  from the public days  well i hope not i think i hope that the  public recognizes like what we are in  and the severity of this crisis  um i'm quite happy to see groups like  extinction rebellion basically saying  i'm not going to be bullied by my own  government and standing up and saying  enough's enough we need action on  climate change and i think all of us  should be part of that  there will be people days who will say  look i have been caught up in those  protests i've been trying to get to work  i have lost a job because i haven't been  able to get to that job interview  there's been incidents where ambulances  haven't been able to get through  and that's where public opinion i  suppose starts to change um what are  your thoughts on that  yeah i think to be honest like it's not  a fun thing to do and i think everyone  thinks we we get like giggles out of it  but that's just not the truth it's  really uncomfortable but then i also  recognize that like one uh we try our  best to mitigate against any  any issues around emergency services uh  the police does do know where we are  broadly because we tell them in advance  so there is logistical planning around  that but then also i think it's  recognizing like you know  there is something about putting  ourselves in the space of discomfort for  the future that comes and like even  though i understand it's very annoying  and i've even been stopped by extinction  rebellion protests but then i also  recognize what is the disruption that's  facing us if we don't address the  climate crisis and what's a disruption  that's already affecting many people  across the world people are dying from  this and we need to start taking it  seriously very quickly  jess some of the tactics used by  extinction rebellion gluing themselves  to lampposts blocking buildings um i'd  like to get your insight into whether  you think a public backlash is starting  to happen  i think the crucial point around this is  the british public fundamentally do want  to see climate action um it's actually  one of the top priorities for the public  at the moment despite you know the  coronavirus pandemic and what's  happening in russia and that's  highlighting again you know the need to  move away from fossil fuels so  fundamentally the public do back um  action and they actually want to see the  uk leading the way to net zero and  making the most of the opportunities  that net zero brings and i mean things  like jobs exports economic growth um all  in communities  that need it um but i think it's it's  you know it's really crucial that it's  the methods um which are divisive and  not the actual issue at hand um it's the  way they go about things rather than you  know people disagreeing with the cause  of the protest well days there's planned  legislation wasn't there in uh the  queen's speech earlier this week um but  said that mean harsher sentencing for  protesters um  you as a climate activist do you think  any of these measures will stop you from  going out and protesting  personally no because i know it's my  right within a democracy  i'm being an active citizen and i think  that's something to be very proud of and  i think all of us should come into  stepping up as active systems in our  democracy and not allowing a lot of the  stuff that is happening in our  government to continue you know where's  the accountability for all of the  ministerial codes that they've broken  where's the accountability for them not  achieving the targets they set  themselves that a lot of science don't  even think it's good enough anyway you  know and it's like if we have to get on  the streets to hold them account and to  go in the history of you know we aren't  the first  movement in the uk to use direct action  we are you know on the you know learning  from the lessons of people like the  suffragettes um so i definitely think if  we are going to start  seeing  legislation being used against us it  actually means we're probably closer to  the finish line than we originally  thought  and and jess briefly it would be  interesting to know how you feel about  this because you mentioned there is a  general public uh welcoming of climate  issues but when i